      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Let $I=N$ and ${S}_{i}=(0,\\frac{2i}{2i-1}),$ then ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}=$",
      "worked_solution": "The intersection of a family of indexed sets $\\{{A}_{i}\\}_{i\\in I}$ contains all elements that belong to *all* sets in our family:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\in U\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{i}\\,\\,\\text{for all }{A}_{i},\\,\\,i\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is a universal set.\n\nLet's now write down a few instances of our indexed sets:\n\n- if $i=1,$ we have ${S}_{1}=(0,2)$\n\n- if $i=2,$ we have ${S}_{2}=(0,\\frac{4}{3})$\n\n- if $i=3,$ we have ${S}_{3}=(0,\\frac{6}{5})$\n\n- if $i=4,$ we have ${S}_{4}=(0,\\frac{8}{7})$\n\nand so on.\n\nNotice that as $i$ increases, \n\n- the left endpoint of the intervals is $0$ for all the sets,\n\n- the right endpoint of the intervals is greater than $1$ and approaches $1$ from the right, never reaching it.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⋂}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}=(0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
